{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KkAkTExLQf4NxUAN8G18kZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→---\n     2→name: plan\n     3→description: Plan a new feature through interactive questioning, producing domain requirements and architecture diagrams.\n     4→disable-model-invocation: true\n     5→argument-hint: <feature-name>\n     6→---\n     7→\n     8→# Vista Plan\n     9→\n    10→Plan a new feature through deep interactive questioning, producing domain requirements and architecture diagrams as the source of truth.\n    11→\n    12→## Invocation\n    13→\n    14→```\n    15→/vista:plan <feature-name>\n    16→```\n    17→\n    18→## Prerequisites\n    19→\n    20→- Project must be the current working directory\n    21→- Python 3.10+ available on PATH\n    22→- Feature name should be lowercase with hyphens (e.g., `user-auth`, `payment-flow`)\n    23→\n    24→## Workflow\n    25→\n    26→### Step 1: Scaffold and Explore\n    27→\n    28→**1a. Scaffold the feature directory:**\n    29→\n    30→```bash\n    31→python vista/scripts/setup-feature.py --name $ARGUMENTS\n    32→```\n    33→\n    34→This creates `.vista/features/<name>/` with:\n    35→- `domain-requirements.md` — Domain requirements template\n    36→- `specs/` — Specification documents directory (populated later by `/vista:specs`)\n    37→- `arch/` — Architecture diagrams directory with empty `_arch.json` manifest\n    38→- `progress.txt` — Progress tracking\n    39→- `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` — Implementation plan (populated by Ralph loops)\n    40→\n    41→If the directory already exists, the script exits gracefully. Proceed to Step 1b.\n    42→\n    43→**1b. Brief codebase exploration:**\n    44→\n    45→Use subagents to quickly understand the project's existing patterns before asking questions.\n    46→\n    47→- **Reference**: `vista/skills/plan/references/discovery-process.md`\n    48→\n    49→Launch 1-3 Explore agents in parallel to discover:\n    50→- **Similar features**: Existing implementations in the same problem domain\n    51→- **Project structure**: Where source code lives, naming conventions, directory organization\n    52→- **Patterns in use**: State management, data access, API patterns, UI frameworks\n    53→\n    54→Present a concise summary of findings to the user. This gives both you and the user shared context before diving into requirements.\n    55→\n    56→### Step 2: Deep Interactive Questioning\n    57→\n    58→This is the core of the plan skill. Use **AskUserQuestion** extensively — ask about every major area until the user is satisfied.\n    59→\n    60→**Reference**: `vista/skills/plan/references/domain-requirements-template.md`\n    61→\n    62→Work through these areas interactively:\n    63→\n    64→#### 2a. Problem & Users\n    65→- What business problem are we solving?\n    66→- Who are the users? (roles, personas, skill levels)\n    67→- What pain points exist today?\n    68→- What outcomes do users expect?\n    69→\n    70→#### 2b. Business Context\n    71→- What are the business objectives?\n    72→- What does success look like? (measurable metrics)\n    73→- Priority/urgency?\n    74→\n    75→#### 2c. Functional Requirements\n    76→- What core functionality must this feature provide?\n    77→- What are the main user workflows? (walk through each step)\n    78→- What data needs to be created, read, updated, deleted?\n    79→- What business rules apply?\n    80→\n    81→#### 2d. Non-Functional Requirements\n    82→- Performance expectations? (response times, throughput)\n    83→- Platform? (web, mobile, desktop, API-only)\n    84→- Offline/online requirements?\n    85→- Security concerns? (auth, data privacy, encryption)\n    86→- Accessibility requirements?\n    87→\n    88→#### 2e. Constraints & Dependencies\n    89→- Technical constraints? (API limits, platform restrictions)\n    90→- Integration requirements? (external APIs, databases, third-party services)\n    91→- Dependencies on other features or systems?\n    92→\n    93→#### 2f. Architecture Concepts\n    94→- What are the major components/services?\n    95→- How do they interact? (data flow, API calls, events)\n    96→- What data entities are involved and how do they relate?\n    97→- What are the key state transitions?\n    98→\n    99→#### 2g. User Experience\n   100→- How do users discover this feature?\n   101→- What's the step-by-step user journey?\n   102→- What feedback mechanisms are needed? (loading states, confirmations, errors)\n   103→- What error scenarios need handling?\n   104→\n   105→**Write answers incrementally** to `.vista/features/<name>/domain-requirements.md` as each area is discussed. Don't wait until the end.\n   106→\n   107→#### 2h. Flag TDD Candidates\n   108→\n   109→As requirements emerge, identify components with heavy logic that would benefit from test-driven development. Look for:\n   110→- Complex calculations or algorithms\n   111→- State machines with many transitions\n   112→- Business rule engines with branching logic\n   113→- Data transformation pipelines\n   114→- Critical path components where bugs have high impact\n   115→\n   116→Add a `## TDD Candidates` section at the end of `domain-requirements.md`:\n   117→\n   118→```markdown\n   119→## TDD Candidates\n   120→\n   121→- **[ComponentName]**: [reason — e.g., \"Complex state machine with 8 states and 15 transitions\"]\n   122→- **[ComponentName]**: [reason — e.g., \"Pricing calculation with discount stacking rules\"]\n   123→```\n   124→\n   125→After questioning is complete, tell the user:\n   126→> Consider running `/vista:tdd <name>` to generate test-first diagrams for flagged components before proceeding to specs.\n   127→\n   128→### Step 3: Generate Architecture Diagrams\n   129→\n   130→Based on the completed `domain-requirements.md`, generate native Mermaid `.mmd` files in `arch/`.\n   131→\n   132→#### 3a. Determine which diagrams to create\n   133→\n   134→Generate diagrams based on the requirements gathered. Typical set:\n   135→\n   136→| Diagram | File | When to Create |\n   137→|---------|------|----------------|\n   138→| System Architecture | `system-architecture.mmd` | Always — shows major components and relationships |\n   139→| User Flow | `user-flow.mmd` | Always — shows primary user journeys as flowchart |\n   140→| Data Model | `data-model.mmd` | When entities/data exist — ER diagram |\n   141→| Sequence Diagrams | `sequence-{flow}.mmd` | For each key interaction flow (typically 2-5) |\n   142→| State Diagrams | `state-{component}.mmd` | For stateful components (if any) |\n   143→\n   144→Not every feature needs every diagram type. Use judgment based on complexity.\n   145→\n   146→#### 3b. Write Mermaid files\n   147→\n   148→Each `.mmd` file must contain valid Mermaid syntax. Follow these conventions:\n   149→\n   150→- Use clear, descriptive node labels\n   151→- Include notes where relationships are non-obvious\n   152→- Group related elements with subgraphs where helpful\n   153→- Keep diagrams focused — one concern per diagram\n   154→\n   155→**Example system architecture:**\n   156→```mermaid\n   157→flowchart TD\n   158→    subgraph Client\n   159→        UI[Web UI]\n   160→        Cache[Local Cache]\n   161→    end\n   162→    subgraph Server\n   163→        API[REST API]\n   164→        Auth[Auth Service]\n   165→        DB[(Database)]\n   166→    end\n   167→    UI --> API\n   168→    API --> Auth\n   169→    API --> DB\n   170→    UI --> Cache\n   171→```\n   172→\n   173→**Example ER diagram:**\n   174→```mermaid\n   175→erDiagram\n   176→    USER ||--o{ ORDER : places\n   177→    ORDER ||--|{ LINE_ITEM : contains\n   178→    PRODUCT ||--o{ LINE_ITEM : \"appears in\"\n   179→    USER {\n   180→        string id PK\n   181→        string email\n   182→        string name\n   183→    }\n   184→    ORDER {\n   185→        string id PK\n   186→        string user_id FK\n   187→        date created_at\n   188→        string status\n   189→    }\n   190→```\n   191→\n   192→#### 3c. Write the manifest\n   193→\n   194→Update `arch/_arch.json` to reference all generated diagrams:\n   195→\n   196→```json\n   197→{\n   198→  \"feature\": \"<name>\",\n   199→  \"diagrams\": [\n   200→    {\n   201→      \"name\": \"System Architecture\",\n   202→      \"file\": \"system-architecture.mmd\",\n   203→      \"type\": \"mermaid\",\n   204→      \"diagramType\": \"flowchart\",\n   205→      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n   206→      \"description\": \"High-level system components and their relationships\"\n   207→    },\n   208→    {\n   209→      \"name\": \"User Flow\",\n   210→      \"file\": \"user-flow.mmd\",\n   211→      \"type\": \"mermaid\",\n   212→      \"diagramType\": \"flowchart\",\n   213→      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n   214→      \"description\": \"Primary user journey through the feature\"\n   215→    }\n   216→  ]\n   217→}\n   218→```\n   219→\n   220→The manifest must conform to `vista/templates/arch-schema.json`.\n   221→\n   222→#### 3d. Report to user\n   223→\n   224→Discover the dashboard URL by calling the `ralph_providers` MCP tool and reading `dashboard_url` from the response.\n   225→\n   226→Tell the user:\n   227→\n   228→1. **Review diagrams** in the web dashboard:\n   229→   ```\n   230→   <dashboard_url>/project/<project-id>/arch/<feature-name>\n   231→   ```\n   232→   Use the AI chat panel to discuss and iterate on diagrams.\n   233→\n   234→2. **Or review directly** by reading the `.mmd` files in the terminal and discussing changes.\n   235→\n   236→3. **Next steps:**\n   237→   - `/vista:tdd <name>` — Generate TDD diagrams for flagged heavy-logic components\n   238→   - `/vista:specs <name>` — Generate detailed specifications from requirements + diagrams\n   239→\n   240→## Output Files\n   241→\n   242→After completion, `.vista/features/<name>/` contains:\n   243→```\n   244→.vista/features/<name>/\n   245→├── arch/\n   246→│   ├── _arch.json              # Diagram manifest\n   247→│   ├── system-architecture.mmd # System components overview\n   248→│   ├── user-flow.mmd           # User journey flowchart\n   249→│   ├── data-model.mmd          # Entity-relationship diagram\n   250→│   ├── sequence-*.mmd          # Interaction sequence diagrams\n   251→│   └── state-*.mmd             # State transition diagrams (if applicable)\n   252→├── specs/                      # Empty (populated by /vista:specs)\n   253→├── domain-requirements.md      # Filled domain requirements with TDD candidates\n   254→├── progress.txt                # Progress tracking\n   255→└── IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md      # Template (populated by Ralph loops)\n   256→```\n   257→\n   258→## References\n   259→\n   260→Reference documents in `vista/skills/plan/references/`:\n   261→- `domain-requirements-template.md` — Template and questioning guide for domain requirements\n   262→- `discovery-process.md` — Guide for codebase exploration with subagents\n   263→- `agent-prompts.md` — Reusable agent prompt templates for discovery\n   264→- `examples.md` — Example plans showing the 3-step workflow\n   265→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d91d5e1b-c8a8-412a-adba-876f560ccc8f","uuid":"1cccda0e-2ee0-4b31-a60d-e4fbf0bd0390","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Grove/OneDrive/Documents/PYTHON/claude_plugins/vista-expanded-visualization/vista/skills/plan/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: plan\ndescription: Plan a new feature through interactive questioning, producing domain requirements and architecture diagrams.\ndisable-model-invocation: true\nargument-hint: <feature-name>\n---\n\n# Vista Plan\n\nPlan a new feature through deep interactive questioning, producing domain requirements and architecture diagrams as the source of truth.\n\n## Invocation\n\n```\n/vista:plan <feature-name>\n```\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- Project must be the current working directory\n- Python 3.10+ available on PATH\n- Feature name should be lowercase with hyphens (e.g., `user-auth`, `payment-flow`)\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Step 1: Scaffold and Explore\n\n**1a. Scaffold the feature directory:**\n\n```bash\npython vista/scripts/setup-feature.py --name $ARGUMENTS\n```\n\nThis creates `.vista/features/<name>/` with:\n- `domain-requirements.md` — Domain requirements template\n- `specs/` — Specification documents directory (populated later by `/vista:specs`)\n- `arch/` — Architecture diagrams directory with empty `_arch.json` manifest\n- `progress.txt` — Progress tracking\n- `IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` — Implementation plan (populated by Ralph loops)\n\nIf the directory already exists, the script exits gracefully. Proceed to Step 1b.\n\n**1b. Brief codebase exploration:**\n\nUse subagents to quickly understand the project's existing patterns before asking questions.\n\n- **Reference**: `vista/skills/plan/references/discovery-process.md`\n\nLaunch 1-3 Explore agents in parallel to discover:\n- **Similar features**: Existing implementations in the same problem domain\n- **Project structure**: Where source code lives, naming conventions, directory organization\n- **Patterns in use**: State management, data access, API patterns, UI frameworks\n\nPresent a concise summary of findings to the user. This gives both you and the user shared context before diving into requirements.\n\n### Step 2: Deep Interactive Questioning\n\nThis is the core of the plan skill. Use **AskUserQuestion** extensively — ask about every major area until the user is satisfied.\n\n**Reference**: `vista/skills/plan/references/domain-requirements-template.md`\n\nWork through these areas interactively:\n\n#### 2a. Problem & Users\n- What business problem are we solving?\n- Who are the users? (roles, personas, skill levels)\n- What pain points exist today?\n- What outcomes do users expect?\n\n#### 2b. Business Context\n- What are the business objectives?\n- What does success look like? (measurable metrics)\n- Priority/urgency?\n\n#### 2c. Functional Requirements\n- What core functionality must this feature provide?\n- What are the main user workflows? (walk through each step)\n- What data needs to be created, read, updated, deleted?\n- What business rules apply?\n\n#### 2d. Non-Functional Requirements\n- Performance expectations? (response times, throughput)\n- Platform? (web, mobile, desktop, API-only)\n- Offline/online requirements?\n- Security concerns? (auth, data privacy, encryption)\n- Accessibility requirements?\n\n#### 2e. Constraints & Dependencies\n- Technical constraints? (API limits, platform restrictions)\n- Integration requirements? (external APIs, databases, third-party services)\n- Dependencies on other features or systems?\n\n#### 2f. Architecture Concepts\n- What are the major components/services?\n- How do they interact? (data flow, API calls, events)\n- What data entities are involved and how do they relate?\n- What are the key state transitions?\n\n#### 2g. User Experience\n- How do users discover this feature?\n- What's the step-by-step user journey?\n- What feedback mechanisms are needed? (loading states, confirmations, errors)\n- What error scenarios need handling?\n\n**Write answers incrementally** to `.vista/features/<name>/domain-requirements.md` as each area is discussed. Don't wait until the end.\n\n#### 2h. Flag TDD Candidates\n\nAs requirements emerge, identify components with heavy logic that would benefit from test-driven development. Look for:\n- Complex calculations or algorithms\n- State machines with many transitions\n- Business rule engines with branching logic\n- Data transformation pipelines\n- Critical path components where bugs have high impact\n\nAdd a `## TDD Candidates` section at the end of `domain-requirements.md`:\n\n```markdown\n## TDD Candidates\n\n- **[ComponentName]**: [reason — e.g., \"Complex state machine with 8 states and 15 transitions\"]\n- **[ComponentName]**: [reason — e.g., \"Pricing calculation with discount stacking rules\"]\n```\n\nAfter questioning is complete, tell the user:\n> Consider running `/vista:tdd <name>` to generate test-first diagrams for flagged components before proceeding to specs.\n\n### Step 3: Generate Architecture Diagrams\n\nBased on the completed `domain-requirements.md`, generate native Mermaid `.mmd` files in `arch/`.\n\n#### 3a. Determine which diagrams to create\n\nGenerate diagrams based on the requirements gathered. Typical set:\n\n| Diagram | File | When to Create |\n|---------|------|----------------|\n| System Architecture | `system-architecture.mmd` | Always — shows major components and relationships |\n| User Flow | `user-flow.mmd` | Always — shows primary user journeys as flowchart |\n| Data Model | `data-model.mmd` | When entities/data exist — ER diagram |\n| Sequence Diagrams | `sequence-{flow}.mmd` | For each key interaction flow (typically 2-5) |\n| State Diagrams | `state-{component}.mmd` | For stateful components (if any) |\n\nNot every feature needs every diagram type. Use judgment based on complexity.\n\n#### 3b. Write Mermaid files\n\nEach `.mmd` file must contain valid Mermaid syntax. Follow these conventions:\n\n- Use clear, descriptive node labels\n- Include notes where relationships are non-obvious\n- Group related elements with subgraphs where helpful\n- Keep diagrams focused — one concern per diagram\n\n**Example system architecture:**\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Client\n        UI[Web UI]\n        Cache[Local Cache]\n    end\n    subgraph Server\n        API[REST API]\n        Auth[Auth Service]\n        DB[(Database)]\n    end\n    UI --> API\n    API --> Auth\n    API --> DB\n    UI --> Cache\n```\n\n**Example ER diagram:**\n```mermaid\nerDiagram\n    USER ||--o{ ORDER : places\n    ORDER ||--|{ LINE_ITEM : contains\n    PRODUCT ||--o{ LINE_ITEM : \"appears in\"\n    USER {\n        string id PK\n        string email\n        string name\n    }\n    ORDER {\n        string id PK\n        string user_id FK\n        date created_at\n        string status\n    }\n```\n\n#### 3c. Write the manifest\n\nUpdate `arch/_arch.json` to reference all generated diagrams:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"feature\": \"<name>\",\n  \"diagrams\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"System Architecture\",\n      \"file\": \"system-architecture.mmd\",\n      \"type\": \"mermaid\",\n      \"diagramType\": \"flowchart\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"description\": \"High-level system components and their relationships\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"User Flow\",\n      \"file\": \"user-flow.mmd\",\n      \"type\": \"mermaid\",\n      \"diagramType\": \"flowchart\",\n      \"category\": \"architecture\",\n      \"description\": \"Primary user journey through the feature\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe manifest must conform to `vista/templates/arch-schema.json`.\n\n#### 3d. Report to user\n\nDiscover the dashboard URL by calling the `ralph_providers` MCP tool and reading `dashboard_url` from the response.\n\nTell the user:\n\n1. **Review diagrams** in the web dashboard:\n   ```\n   <dashboard_url>/project/<project-id>/arch/<feature-name>\n   ```\n   Use the AI chat panel to discuss and iterate on diagrams.\n\n2. **Or review directly** by reading the `.mmd` files in the terminal and discussing changes.\n\n3. **Next steps:**\n   - `/vista:tdd <name>` — Generate TDD diagrams for flagged heavy-logic components\n   - `/vista:specs <name>` — Generate detailed specifications from requirements + diagrams\n\n## Output Files\n\nAfter completion, `.vista/features/<name>/` contains:\n```\n.vista/features/<name>/\n├── arch/\n│   ├── _arch.json              # Diagram manifest\n│   ├── system-architecture.mmd # System components overview\n│   ├── user-flow.mmd           # User journey flowchart\n│   ├── data-model.mmd          # Entity-relationship diagram\n│   ├── sequence-*.mmd          # Interaction sequence diagrams\n│   └── state-*.mmd             # State transition diagrams (if applicable)\n├── specs/                      # Empty (populated by /vista:specs)\n├── domain-requirements.md      # Filled domain requirements with TDD candidates\n├── progress.txt                # Progress tracking\n└── IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md      # Template (populated by Ralph loops)\n```\n\n## References\n\nReference documents in `vista/skills/plan/references/`:\n- `domain-requirements-template.md` — Template and questioning guide for domain requirements\n- `discovery-process.md` — Guide for codebase exploration with subagents\n- `agent-prompts.md` — Reusable agent prompt templates for discovery\n- `examples.md` — Example plans showing the 3-step workflow\n","numLines":265,"startLine":1,"totalLines":265}}}
