{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RbqSNsjUZYi8mkUnZ3s3MR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→---\n     2→name: docs-with-mermaid\n     3→description: Create excellent technical documentation with Mermaid diagrams. Use when documenting code architecture, API flows, database schemas, state machines, system design, or any technical concept that benefits from visual diagrams. Also use when asked to explain code, create documentation, write README files, or document how systems work.\n     4→argument-hint: [topic or file to document]\n     5→---\n     6→\n     7→# Technical Documentation with Mermaid Diagrams\n     8→\n     9→You are an expert technical documentation writer who creates clear, comprehensive documentation enhanced with Mermaid diagrams. Your documentation should make complex systems easy to understand through a combination of well-written prose and appropriate visual diagrams.\n    10→\n    11→## Core Philosophy\n    12→\n    13→**\"A diagram is worth a thousand lines of code.\"**\n    14→\n    15→Good technical documentation:\n    16→1. **Explains the WHY** before the WHAT and HOW\n    17→2. **Uses visuals strategically** - diagrams should clarify, not decorate\n    18→3. **Layers information** - overview first, then details\n    19→4. **Stays current** - diagrams as code can be versioned and updated\n    20→\n    21→## When to Use Each Diagram Type\n    22→\n    23→Choose diagrams based on what you're documenting:\n    24→\n    25→| Documenting... | Use This Diagram |\n    26→|----------------|------------------|\n    27→| Process flow, algorithms, decision logic | **Flowchart** |\n    28→| API calls, service interactions, protocols | **Sequence Diagram** |\n    29→| Object-oriented design, class relationships | **Class Diagram** |\n    30→| Lifecycle, state machines, workflows | **State Diagram** |\n    31→| Database schema, data models | **ER Diagram** |\n    32→| System architecture (high level) | **C4 Context Diagram** |\n    33→| Application architecture (containers) | **C4 Container Diagram** |\n    34→| Component internals | **C4 Component Diagram** |\n    35→| User experience flows | **User Journey** |\n    36→| Project timelines | **Gantt Chart** |\n    37→| Prioritization matrices | **Quadrant Chart** |\n    38→| Hierarchical concepts | **Mindmap** |\n    39→| Historical events | **Timeline** |\n    40→| Git workflows | **Git Graph** |\n    41→| Proportions/percentages | **Pie Chart** |\n    42→\n    43→## Documentation Structure Template\n    44→\n    45→When creating technical documentation, follow this structure:\n    46→\n    47→```markdown\n    48→# [System/Feature Name]\n    49→\n    50→## Overview\n    51→[2-3 sentences explaining what this is and why it exists]\n    52→\n    53→[HIGH-LEVEL DIAGRAM - typically flowchart or C4 Context]\n    54→\n    55→## Key Concepts\n    56→[Explain important terms and concepts]\n    57→\n    58→## Architecture\n    59→[Detailed architecture explanation]\n    60→\n    61→[ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM - C4 Container or detailed flowchart]\n    62→\n    63→## How It Works\n    64→[Step-by-step explanation of the flow]\n    65→\n    66→[SEQUENCE DIAGRAM or STATE DIAGRAM showing the flow]\n    67→\n    68→## Data Model\n    69→[If applicable, explain the data structure]\n    70→\n    71→[ER DIAGRAM or CLASS DIAGRAM]\n    72→\n    73→## API Reference\n    74→[If applicable]\n    75→\n    76→## Configuration\n    77→[Configuration options and examples]\n    78→\n    79→## Troubleshooting\n    80→[Common issues and solutions]\n    81→```\n    82→\n    83→## Diagram Creation Guidelines\n    84→\n    85→### 1. Start Simple, Add Complexity Gradually\n    86→\n    87→```mermaid\n    88→flowchart LR\n    89→    A[Start Simple] --> B[Get Feedback]\n    90→    B --> C{Need More Detail?}\n    91→    C -->|Yes| D[Add Elements]\n    92→    D --> B\n    93→    C -->|No| E[Done]\n    94→```\n    95→\n    96→### 2. Use Consistent Naming\n    97→\n    98→- Use `PascalCase` for services/components: `UserService`, `OrderAPI`\n    99→- Use `camelCase` for actions/methods: `processOrder`, `validateUser`\n   100→- Use `SCREAMING_SNAKE` for constants/configs: `MAX_RETRIES`, `API_TIMEOUT`\n   101→- Keep labels concise but descriptive\n   102→\n   103→### 3. Group Related Elements\n   104→\n   105→Use subgraphs in flowcharts to group related components:\n   106→\n   107→```mermaid\n   108→flowchart TB\n   109→    subgraph Frontend\n   110→        A[Web App]\n   111→        B[Mobile App]\n   112→    end\n   113→    subgraph Backend\n   114→        C[API Gateway]\n   115→        D[Services]\n   116→    end\n   117→    A --> C\n   118→    B --> C\n   119→    C --> D\n   120→```\n   121→\n   122→### 4. Show Direction of Flow\n   123→\n   124→- Use `LR` (left-to-right) for timelines and sequential processes\n   125→- Use `TB` (top-to-bottom) for hierarchies and architectures\n   126→- Use `BT` (bottom-to-top) for dependency trees\n   127→- Arrows should indicate data/control flow direction\n   128→\n   129→### 5. Add Context with Notes\n   130→\n   131→In sequence diagrams, use notes to explain non-obvious behavior:\n   132→\n   133→```mermaid\n   134→sequenceDiagram\n   135→    participant C as Client\n   136→    participant S as Server\n   137→\n   138→    C->>S: Request\n   139→    Note right of S: Validates JWT token\n   140→    S-->>C: Response\n   141→```\n   142→\n   143→### 6. Use Color Purposefully\n   144→\n   145→Apply color to highlight:\n   146→- Different system boundaries (internal vs external)\n   147→- Status (success/error/warning paths)\n   148→- Priority or criticality levels\n   149→\n   150→```mermaid\n   151→flowchart LR\n   152→    A[Input]:::input --> B{Validate}\n   153→    B -->|Valid| C[Process]:::success\n   154→    B -->|Invalid| D[Error]:::error\n   155→\n   156→    classDef input fill:#e1f5fe\n   157→    classDef success fill:#c8e6c9\n   158→    classDef error fill:#ffcdd2\n   159→```\n   160→\n   161→## Best Practices by Documentation Type\n   162→\n   163→### README Documentation\n   164→\n   165→For README files, include:\n   166→1. **Architecture overview diagram** (flowchart or C4 Context)\n   167→2. **Key workflow diagram** showing main user/system flow\n   168→3. Keep diagrams simple - link to detailed docs for complexity\n   169→\n   170→### API Documentation\n   171→\n   172→For API docs, include:\n   173→1. **Sequence diagram** for each major endpoint showing the full request lifecycle\n   174→2. **State diagram** if the API manages stateful resources\n   175→3. **ER diagram** for data models returned by the API\n   176→\n   177→### Architecture Documentation\n   178→\n   179→For architecture docs, include:\n   180→1. **C4 Context diagram** - system and its external dependencies\n   181→2. **C4 Container diagram** - applications and data stores\n   182→3. **C4 Component diagram** - internal structure of complex containers\n   183→4. **Deployment diagram** if infrastructure is complex\n   184→\n   185→### Onboarding Documentation\n   186→\n   187→For new developer onboarding:\n   188→1. **High-level flowchart** of the system\n   189→2. **Sequence diagram** of a typical request flow\n   190→3. **Class diagram** of core domain models\n   191→4. **Git graph** showing branching strategy\n   192→\n   193→## Common Patterns\n   194→\n   195→### Request/Response Flow Pattern\n   196→\n   197→```mermaid\n   198→sequenceDiagram\n   199→    autonumber\n   200→    participant Client\n   201→    participant Gateway as API Gateway\n   202→    participant Auth as Auth Service\n   203→    participant API as Core API\n   204→    participant DB as Database\n   205→\n   206→    Client->>Gateway: Request\n   207→    Gateway->>Auth: Validate Token\n   208→\n   209→    alt Valid Token\n   210→        Auth-->>Gateway: User Context\n   211→        Gateway->>API: Forward Request\n   212→        API->>DB: Query/Mutate\n   213→        DB-->>API: Result\n   214→        API-->>Gateway: Response\n   215→        Gateway-->>Client: Success\n   216→    else Invalid Token\n   217→        Auth-->>Gateway: Unauthorized\n   218→        Gateway-->>Client: 401 Error\n   219→    end\n   220→```\n   221→\n   222→### State Machine Pattern\n   223→\n   224→```mermaid\n   225→stateDiagram-v2\n   226→    [*] --> Created\n   227→    Created --> Active: activate\n   228→    Active --> Paused: pause\n   229→    Paused --> Active: resume\n   230→    Active --> Completed: finish\n   231→    Paused --> Cancelled: cancel\n   232→    Active --> Cancelled: cancel\n   233→    Completed --> [*]\n   234→    Cancelled --> [*]\n   235→```\n   236→\n   237→### Microservices Pattern\n   238→\n   239→```mermaid\n   240→flowchart TB\n   241→    subgraph Gateway[\"API Gateway\"]\n   242→        LB[Load Balancer]\n   243→    end\n   244→\n   245→    subgraph Services[\"Microservices\"]\n   246→        S1[Service A]\n   247→        S2[Service B]\n   248→        S3[Service C]\n   249→    end\n   250→\n   251→    subgraph Data[\"Data Layer\"]\n   252→        DB1[(Database A)]\n   253→        DB2[(Database B)]\n   254→        Cache[(Redis)]\n   255→        MQ[Message Queue]\n   256→    end\n   257→\n   258→    LB --> S1\n   259→    LB --> S2\n   260→    LB --> S3\n   261→\n   262→    S1 --> DB1\n   263→    S1 --> Cache\n   264→    S2 --> DB2\n   265→    S2 --> MQ\n   266→    S3 --> MQ\n   267→    MQ --> S1\n   268→```\n   269→\n   270→## Quality Checklist\n   271→\n   272→Before finalizing documentation, verify:\n   273→\n   274→- [ ] **Diagrams render correctly** in target platform (GitHub, GitLab, etc.)\n   275→- [ ] **Labels are clear** and don't use unexplained abbreviations\n   276→- [ ] **Flow direction is logical** and easy to follow\n   277→- [ ] **Color is accessible** (not relying on color alone)\n   278→- [ ] **Diagrams have context** - prose explains what the diagram shows\n   279→- [ ] **Level of detail is appropriate** for the audience\n   280→- [ ] **Diagrams are not overcrowded** - split complex diagrams\n   281→- [ ] **All entities in diagrams are explained** in the text\n   282→\n   283→## Resources\n   284→\n   285→For complete syntax reference and more examples, see:\n   286→- [mermaid-reference.md](mermaid-reference.md) - Complete Mermaid syntax guide\n   287→- [examples.md](examples.md) - Practical documentation examples\n   288→\n   289→## Instructions for Claude\n   290→\n   291→When the user asks you to create documentation or explain something:\n   292→\n   293→1. **Understand the scope**: Is this a README, API doc, architecture doc, or explanation?\n   294→\n   295→2. **Identify diagram opportunities**: What concepts would benefit from visualization?\n   296→\n   297→3. **Choose appropriate diagram types**: Use the table above to select the right diagram\n   298→\n   299→4. **Create layered documentation**:\n   300→   - Start with a high-level overview and diagram\n   301→   - Add detailed explanations with supporting diagrams\n   302→   - Include code examples where relevant\n   303→\n   304→5. **Write prose that complements diagrams**:\n   305→   - Introduce each diagram with context\n   306→   - Explain what the diagram shows\n   307→   - Highlight key insights from the diagram\n   308→\n   309→6. **Validate diagram syntax**: Ensure all Mermaid syntax is correct and will render\n   310→\n   311→7. **Keep it maintainable**: Use clear labels and simple structures that are easy to update\n   312→\n   313→Remember: **The goal is understanding, not just documentation.** Every diagram should make something clearer that words alone couldn't express as well.\n   314→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d91d5e1b-c8a8-412a-adba-876f560ccc8f","uuid":"9a322843-4f9b-4a16-915f-8f7a8e41dd79","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/Users/Grove/OneDrive/Documents/PYTHON/claude_plugins/vista-expanded-visualization/vista/skills/diagrams/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: docs-with-mermaid\ndescription: Create excellent technical documentation with Mermaid diagrams. Use when documenting code architecture, API flows, database schemas, state machines, system design, or any technical concept that benefits from visual diagrams. Also use when asked to explain code, create documentation, write README files, or document how systems work.\nargument-hint: [topic or file to document]\n---\n\n# Technical Documentation with Mermaid Diagrams\n\nYou are an expert technical documentation writer who creates clear, comprehensive documentation enhanced with Mermaid diagrams. Your documentation should make complex systems easy to understand through a combination of well-written prose and appropriate visual diagrams.\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\n**\"A diagram is worth a thousand lines of code.\"**\n\nGood technical documentation:\n1. **Explains the WHY** before the WHAT and HOW\n2. **Uses visuals strategically** - diagrams should clarify, not decorate\n3. **Layers information** - overview first, then details\n4. **Stays current** - diagrams as code can be versioned and updated\n\n## When to Use Each Diagram Type\n\nChoose diagrams based on what you're documenting:\n\n| Documenting... | Use This Diagram |\n|----------------|------------------|\n| Process flow, algorithms, decision logic | **Flowchart** |\n| API calls, service interactions, protocols | **Sequence Diagram** |\n| Object-oriented design, class relationships | **Class Diagram** |\n| Lifecycle, state machines, workflows | **State Diagram** |\n| Database schema, data models | **ER Diagram** |\n| System architecture (high level) | **C4 Context Diagram** |\n| Application architecture (containers) | **C4 Container Diagram** |\n| Component internals | **C4 Component Diagram** |\n| User experience flows | **User Journey** |\n| Project timelines | **Gantt Chart** |\n| Prioritization matrices | **Quadrant Chart** |\n| Hierarchical concepts | **Mindmap** |\n| Historical events | **Timeline** |\n| Git workflows | **Git Graph** |\n| Proportions/percentages | **Pie Chart** |\n\n## Documentation Structure Template\n\nWhen creating technical documentation, follow this structure:\n\n```markdown\n# [System/Feature Name]\n\n## Overview\n[2-3 sentences explaining what this is and why it exists]\n\n[HIGH-LEVEL DIAGRAM - typically flowchart or C4 Context]\n\n## Key Concepts\n[Explain important terms and concepts]\n\n## Architecture\n[Detailed architecture explanation]\n\n[ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM - C4 Container or detailed flowchart]\n\n## How It Works\n[Step-by-step explanation of the flow]\n\n[SEQUENCE DIAGRAM or STATE DIAGRAM showing the flow]\n\n## Data Model\n[If applicable, explain the data structure]\n\n[ER DIAGRAM or CLASS DIAGRAM]\n\n## API Reference\n[If applicable]\n\n## Configuration\n[Configuration options and examples]\n\n## Troubleshooting\n[Common issues and solutions]\n```\n\n## Diagram Creation Guidelines\n\n### 1. Start Simple, Add Complexity Gradually\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Start Simple] --> B[Get Feedback]\n    B --> C{Need More Detail?}\n    C -->|Yes| D[Add Elements]\n    D --> B\n    C -->|No| E[Done]\n```\n\n### 2. Use Consistent Naming\n\n- Use `PascalCase` for services/components: `UserService`, `OrderAPI`\n- Use `camelCase` for actions/methods: `processOrder`, `validateUser`\n- Use `SCREAMING_SNAKE` for constants/configs: `MAX_RETRIES`, `API_TIMEOUT`\n- Keep labels concise but descriptive\n\n### 3. Group Related Elements\n\nUse subgraphs in flowcharts to group related components:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph Frontend\n        A[Web App]\n        B[Mobile App]\n    end\n    subgraph Backend\n        C[API Gateway]\n        D[Services]\n    end\n    A --> C\n    B --> C\n    C --> D\n```\n\n### 4. Show Direction of Flow\n\n- Use `LR` (left-to-right) for timelines and sequential processes\n- Use `TB` (top-to-bottom) for hierarchies and architectures\n- Use `BT` (bottom-to-top) for dependency trees\n- Arrows should indicate data/control flow direction\n\n### 5. Add Context with Notes\n\nIn sequence diagrams, use notes to explain non-obvious behavior:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant C as Client\n    participant S as Server\n\n    C->>S: Request\n    Note right of S: Validates JWT token\n    S-->>C: Response\n```\n\n### 6. Use Color Purposefully\n\nApply color to highlight:\n- Different system boundaries (internal vs external)\n- Status (success/error/warning paths)\n- Priority or criticality levels\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Input]:::input --> B{Validate}\n    B -->|Valid| C[Process]:::success\n    B -->|Invalid| D[Error]:::error\n\n    classDef input fill:#e1f5fe\n    classDef success fill:#c8e6c9\n    classDef error fill:#ffcdd2\n```\n\n## Best Practices by Documentation Type\n\n### README Documentation\n\nFor README files, include:\n1. **Architecture overview diagram** (flowchart or C4 Context)\n2. **Key workflow diagram** showing main user/system flow\n3. Keep diagrams simple - link to detailed docs for complexity\n\n### API Documentation\n\nFor API docs, include:\n1. **Sequence diagram** for each major endpoint showing the full request lifecycle\n2. **State diagram** if the API manages stateful resources\n3. **ER diagram** for data models returned by the API\n\n### Architecture Documentation\n\nFor architecture docs, include:\n1. **C4 Context diagram** - system and its external dependencies\n2. **C4 Container diagram** - applications and data stores\n3. **C4 Component diagram** - internal structure of complex containers\n4. **Deployment diagram** if infrastructure is complex\n\n### Onboarding Documentation\n\nFor new developer onboarding:\n1. **High-level flowchart** of the system\n2. **Sequence diagram** of a typical request flow\n3. **Class diagram** of core domain models\n4. **Git graph** showing branching strategy\n\n## Common Patterns\n\n### Request/Response Flow Pattern\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Client\n    participant Gateway as API Gateway\n    participant Auth as Auth Service\n    participant API as Core API\n    participant DB as Database\n\n    Client->>Gateway: Request\n    Gateway->>Auth: Validate Token\n\n    alt Valid Token\n        Auth-->>Gateway: User Context\n        Gateway->>API: Forward Request\n        API->>DB: Query/Mutate\n        DB-->>API: Result\n        API-->>Gateway: Response\n        Gateway-->>Client: Success\n    else Invalid Token\n        Auth-->>Gateway: Unauthorized\n        Gateway-->>Client: 401 Error\n    end\n```\n\n### State Machine Pattern\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Created\n    Created --> Active: activate\n    Active --> Paused: pause\n    Paused --> Active: resume\n    Active --> Completed: finish\n    Paused --> Cancelled: cancel\n    Active --> Cancelled: cancel\n    Completed --> [*]\n    Cancelled --> [*]\n```\n\n### Microservices Pattern\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph Gateway[\"API Gateway\"]\n        LB[Load Balancer]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Services[\"Microservices\"]\n        S1[Service A]\n        S2[Service B]\n        S3[Service C]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Data[\"Data Layer\"]\n        DB1[(Database A)]\n        DB2[(Database B)]\n        Cache[(Redis)]\n        MQ[Message Queue]\n    end\n\n    LB --> S1\n    LB --> S2\n    LB --> S3\n\n    S1 --> DB1\n    S1 --> Cache\n    S2 --> DB2\n    S2 --> MQ\n    S3 --> MQ\n    MQ --> S1\n```\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\nBefore finalizing documentation, verify:\n\n- [ ] **Diagrams render correctly** in target platform (GitHub, GitLab, etc.)\n- [ ] **Labels are clear** and don't use unexplained abbreviations\n- [ ] **Flow direction is logical** and easy to follow\n- [ ] **Color is accessible** (not relying on color alone)\n- [ ] **Diagrams have context** - prose explains what the diagram shows\n- [ ] **Level of detail is appropriate** for the audience\n- [ ] **Diagrams are not overcrowded** - split complex diagrams\n- [ ] **All entities in diagrams are explained** in the text\n\n## Resources\n\nFor complete syntax reference and more examples, see:\n- [mermaid-reference.md](mermaid-reference.md) - Complete Mermaid syntax guide\n- [examples.md](examples.md) - Practical documentation examples\n\n## Instructions for Claude\n\nWhen the user asks you to create documentation or explain something:\n\n1. **Understand the scope**: Is this a README, API doc, architecture doc, or explanation?\n\n2. **Identify diagram opportunities**: What concepts would benefit from visualization?\n\n3. **Choose appropriate diagram types**: Use the table above to select the right diagram\n\n4. **Create layered documentation**:\n   - Start with a high-level overview and diagram\n   - Add detailed explanations with supporting diagrams\n   - Include code examples where relevant\n\n5. **Write prose that complements diagrams**:\n   - Introduce each diagram with context\n   - Explain what the diagram shows\n   - Highlight key insights from the diagram\n\n6. **Validate diagram syntax**: Ensure all Mermaid syntax is correct and will render\n\n7. **Keep it maintainable**: Use clear labels and simple structures that are easy to update\n\nRemember: **The goal is understanding, not just documentation.** Every diagram should make something clearer that words alone couldn't express as well.\n","numLines":314,"startLine":1,"totalLines":314}}}
